Chapter: RealSaturatingFinite

  cites Foreword chapter Console

 THE FINITE CASE IS THE ONE NOBODY TESTS. A saturating operation is written
 for the overflow, so every test written for it overflows, and the arm that
 never runs is the ordinary one where the clamp must do nothing and get out
 of the way. `real-approx-modes` has three overflowing lines and one finite
 line, and for a while the finite line was the only thing standing between
 this and shipping.

 What it caught, measured 2026-08-19 on the arm64 plug: a finite saturating
 multiply returned its LEFT OPERAND. `sat64 2 3` answered 2 and `sat64 5 0`
 answered 5, while the same expressions were right on riscv64 and right on
 x86. The clamp emitter ends with a conditional skip over the instruction
 that writes the result, a peephole had folded the return-register move into
 that skipped instruction, and so the answer was correct only when the
 result overflowed to an infinity.

 Every line below is finite, every answer is a whole number, and on no line
 does the answer equal either operand. Add, subtract and multiply are given
 in both argument orders so that returning the left operand and returning
 the right one are distinguished rather than merely detected. An emitter
 that ignores the clamp entirely passes all nine, which is correct: doing
 nothing to a finite result IS the requirement here.

Section: Operations

  sat-add : Real, Real -> Real saturating
  sat-add (a) (b) = to-real-saturating a + to-real-saturating b

  sat-sub : Real, Real -> Real saturating
  sat-sub (a) (b) = to-real-saturating a - to-real-saturating b

  sat-mul : Real, Real -> Real saturating
  sat-mul (a) (b) = to-real-saturating a * to-real-saturating b

  sat-div : Real, Real -> Real saturating
  sat-div (a) (b) = to-real-saturating a / to-real-saturating b

  sat-int : Real saturating -> Integer
  sat-int (x) = real-to-int (from-real-saturating x)

Section: Report

 Bit patterns are not needed here and would hide the arithmetic: these are
 small whole numbers and an integer conversion reads them exactly.

  opening : [Console] Nothing
  opening = act
    print-line-uni ("add 8 3 " & show (sat-int (sat-add (real-from-int 8) (real-from-int 3))))
    print-line-uni ("add 3 8 " & show (sat-int (sat-add (real-from-int 3) (real-from-int 8))))
    print-line-uni ("sub 8 3 " & show (sat-int (sat-sub (real-from-int 8) (real-from-int 3))))
    print-line-uni ("sub 3 8 " & show (sat-int (sat-sub (real-from-int 3) (real-from-int 8))))
    print-line-uni ("mul 8 3 " & show (sat-int (sat-mul (real-from-int 8) (real-from-int 3))))
    print-line-uni ("mul 3 8 " & show (sat-int (sat-mul (real-from-int 3) (real-from-int 8))))
    print-line-uni ("div 8 4 " & show (sat-int (sat-div (real-from-int 8) (real-from-int 4))))
    print-line-uni ("div 24 8 " & show (sat-int (sat-div (real-from-int 24) (real-from-int 8))))
    print-line-uni ("mul 5 0 " & show (sat-int (sat-mul (real-from-int 5) (real-from-int 0))))
  end
